WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:02.980
I've long been fascinated
with college sports.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.304
I grew up down south where
college football is a religion.

00:00:06.304 --> 00:00:10.020
I saw up close the
power of Hoya basketball

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.432
when I went to Georgetown.

00:00:11.432 --> 00:00:14.768
And now I've got a son set
to play college lacrosse,

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:18.230
and that's giving me a whole
new window into that world.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.840
And as with everything,
I see a business story.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.960
Right now a first year lacrosse player

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.230
at a division three school like my son

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:27.310
and Trevor Lawrence,

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.100
Clemson's championship winning quarterback

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.904
and widely touted NFL star,

00:00:30.904 --> 00:00:32.984
well, they have something in common.

00:00:32.984 --> 00:00:35.100
Neither of them are
allowed to be paid a dime

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.610
while they play sports
at the college level.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.240
And that may be about to change.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.780
I think 2021 will be a pivotal year

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.460
for college sports for a lot of reasons.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.830
We've been talking about

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.450
the potential tipping
point in college sports.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:49.840
This may be the time.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:50.893
With the pandemic,

00:00:50.893 --> 00:00:52.708
with the racial justice issues,

00:00:52.708 --> 00:00:55.718
with the activity at the
federal and state level,

00:00:55.718 --> 00:00:58.316
with the activity at the Supreme Court,

00:00:58.316 --> 00:01:01.797
that we say, this is no
longer a sustainable model.

00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:04.610
Schools are conspiring with competitors,

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:05.920
agreeing with competitors,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.270
to pay no salaries to the workers

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.710
who are making the schools
billions of dollars

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.270
on the theory that
consumers want the schools

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.410
to pay their workers nothing.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.170
After years of asking the question,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.980
will paying college athletes
destroy college athletics?

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.296
It looks like finally in 2021,

00:01:22.296 --> 00:01:24.244
that question will be answered.

00:01:24.244 --> 00:01:26.583
The tipping point is here.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.004
College sports was built
on the idea of amateurism.

00:01:38.004 --> 00:01:39.750
More than a hundred years ago,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.264
after the death of 18 students
and injuries to 150 others,

00:01:43.264 --> 00:01:45.867
the NCAA was formed as a nonprofit.

00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:48.970
Its mission: to protect the
lives of football players

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.100
by adding new rules to the sport.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.000
The biggest being the
advent of the forward pass.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.340
One of the core tenets:

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.357
these were not professional athletes.

00:01:57.357 --> 00:02:01.962
Amateurism is defined in
the NCAA's 465 page manual

00:02:01.962 --> 00:02:04.568
as being motivated primarily by education

00:02:04.568 --> 00:02:07.610
and by the physical,
mental, and social benefits

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.840
to be derived,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.960
protected from exploitation
by professional

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.583
and commercial enterprises.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.000
In lieu of paying the players,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.500
schools provide athletes
with scholarships.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.335
But today, between the college
football championships-

00:02:21.335 --> 00:02:22.343
Touchdown.

00:02:22.343 --> 00:02:24.915
DeVonta Smith cannot be stopped.

00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:26.640
And March Madness-

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.937
How about that?

00:02:27.937 --> 00:02:30.940
The NCAA is a money-making machine,

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.022
with nearly $19 billion
in revenue in 2019 alone.

00:02:35.022 --> 00:02:37.823
And the students, they
still don't get paid.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.590
When and how did it get so out of control?

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.850
Like what was there a catalytic moment

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.511
or was this just a slow bleed as it were?

00:02:49.511 --> 00:02:54.410
The explosion in revenues
came from another

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:59.410
Supreme Court antitrust case
called Board of Regents 37 ago.

00:02:59.742 --> 00:03:02.243
Jeffrey Kessler is the co-
lead counsel for the basketball

00:03:02.243 --> 00:03:05.330
and football players
who are suing the NCAA

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.480
for greater economic rights.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.669
What happened in Board of Regents

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:14.470
is the Supreme Court ruled
that the NCAA could not

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:18.560
restrict the broadcasts
of college football

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.213
and had to allow the conferences

00:03:20.213 --> 00:03:23.300
and the schools to
compete with each other.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.300
That led to an explosion
in television revenues,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.661
both in college football
and then derivatively

00:03:29.661 --> 00:03:31.663
in college basketball.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.280
Rather than two networks broadcasting

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.845
a total of 14 college
football games each week,

00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:40.080
more than 15 networks
carry nearly every college

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.010
football game on TV.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.770
More games led to more ad
revenue and events like-

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.803
LSU sits on the throne
of college football.

00:03:48.803 --> 00:03:50.940
The college football championship

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.950
brings in close to half a billion dollars

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.339
in revenue every year.

00:03:54.339 --> 00:03:56.099
That revenue ends up influencing

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:58.653
how college sports is organized.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.340
There was a great kind of realignment,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.605
if you will, of a number
of the major conferences.

00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:07.930
This is Amy Privette Perko,

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.770
executive director of
the Knight Commission,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.770
an independent group
that spearheads reform

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.023
in college athletics.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.001
And that was really done
to increase the footprint

00:04:17.001 --> 00:04:21.370
of those conferences for
the football TV market.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.570
And what may work for
the football TV market

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.010
may not be the best solution

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.560
for all the other sport programs.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.393
While there are more

00:04:31.393 --> 00:04:33.480
than 350 division one basketball schools,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.420
they're only 130 in football,

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.170
also known as the football
bowl subdivision, or FBS.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.640
That's led to a great imbalance

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.594
in schools' athletic budgets.

00:04:42.594 --> 00:04:46.197
FBS college football is
the most powerful sport

00:04:46.197 --> 00:04:50.210
in college sports from the
standpoint of the finances

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.053
and shaping the entire landscape.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.200
Because of the money in football,

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.215
you have athletic programs in division one

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:02.560
whose budgets are $5 million a year,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.440
and they're competing in the
same division in basketball

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.663
with universities that have
athletic department budgets

00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:11.780
of more than $200 million a year.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.900
College football already rakes in money

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:14.760
hand over fist,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.980
but the new playoff
system is about to make it

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.650
more profitable than ever.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.540
According to CNN, the
64 schools that compete

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.380
for a spot in that final game

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.695
brought in a combined
$2.8 billion last season.

00:05:27.695 --> 00:05:29.800
And with billions of dollars now coming

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.810
into NCAA programs, that
money needs to be spent.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.410
The phrase they use in economics

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:36.951
is rent seeking behavior.

00:05:36.951 --> 00:05:39.250
This is my colleague, Joe Nocera,

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.180
a Bloomberg opinion writer and author,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.820
who's been delving into the inequalities

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.550
of college sports for more than a decade.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:48.050
That means money has to go somewhere.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:49.470
And with division one basketball

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:50.880
and football being the lion's share

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.720
of where the revenue comes from,

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.630
the money goes straight
back into those programs.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.670
Where's it going to go?

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.230
It goes to the administrators.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.220
It goes to the conference commissioners.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.130
It goes to the coaches.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.510
It goes to the assistant coaches.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:04.812
It goes to the weight coaches.

00:06:04.812 --> 00:06:07.320
It goes to these fancy buildings.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.030
It goes to a new football stadium.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.410
Nick Saban makes north of

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.567
five and a half million dollars a year.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:17.460
Very simply, is he worth it
to the University of Alabama?

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.290
Nick Saban's the best financial investment

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:22.160
this University has ever made.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.210
About 45 of the 50 states where either

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.900
the basketball coach or the football coach

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.650
is the highest paid state employee.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.660
However, there is one place

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.960
that money does not go.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.070
They all say, well,

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:35.659
we can't afford to pay the players.

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:38.750
Of course you can afford
to pay the players.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.990
You've just chosen not to.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:41.823
For years,

00:06:41.823 --> 00:06:44.200
the NCAA has made two main arguments.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.033
The first-

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:46.670
Well, they are paid.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.100
We're here to educate them
and help them grow as people,

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.130
but we're not here to help them

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.900
in terms of their financial gain.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.330
The second being that paying players

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.420
would take away from college sports quote,

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:56.831
spirit of amateurism,

00:06:56.831 --> 00:06:59.570
and some people do agree
with those sentiments,

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:00.963
even some of the players.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.820
I think on the financial side,

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.990
what the influx of, you know,

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.100
monetary incentives has done

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.460
is it's just made that process
a little bit harder, right?

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:14.650
This is Kendall Spencer,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:17.360
former track star at the
University of New Mexico,

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.110
Olympic hopeful and the
first student to ever sit

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.159
on the NCAA division one board.

00:07:22.159 --> 00:07:26.110
I reflect on just
ideally what I would want

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.100
a student athlete to be thinking about

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.940
when they were choosing
their institution was

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.550
you know, where can I go

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.275
to not only get this
great athletic experience,

00:07:34.275 --> 00:07:36.570
and you know, maybe I'm
trying to make an Olympic team

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.340
or maybe I'm trying to make
the professional leagues,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.180
but beyond that, where can I go

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.180
where I get what I need academically?

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.940
So that no matter what happens,
I'm going to be successful.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:47.980
Kendall and others argue

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.480
that this is actually what
the current model accomplishes

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:53.040
for the vast, vast majority of athletes.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.290
They say that reforms
shouldn't unduly punish

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.370
those true student athletes in the process

00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:59.760
of reforming a system
that's been undermined

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.830
by two big money sports.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.540
So for some, a strong
education really is the

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.670
best compensation they can get
for playing college sports.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.208
But the money is hard to ignore,

00:08:09.208 --> 00:08:11.803
and the pandemic made it even harder.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.370
Tonight, the NCAA president says

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:16.950
there will be no fall championships

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.060
except for the college football playoff.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.610
The money was so important
for the universities.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.070
They were going to play football,
come hell or high water.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.852
It didn't really matter

00:08:27.852 --> 00:08:30.350
if the athletes got COVID.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:31.430
They were going to play.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:32.890
They'd always said, you know,

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:36.343
they're students first and
you know, athletes second.

00:08:36.343 --> 00:08:39.391
And they're there for an education.

00:08:39.391 --> 00:08:42.643
So here you had a situation
where most of the rest

00:08:42.643 --> 00:08:46.950
of the university, in many
cases, was not even on campus.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.440
We reached out to the NCAA

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:50.271
about the health risks posed to players,

00:08:50.271 --> 00:08:52.343
but they didn't respond.

00:08:52.343 --> 00:08:55.080
And critics say that the
canceling of one sport

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.120
but the continuation of another solely

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:58.800
because of financial reasons

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.520
makes the NCAAs amateurism argument moot.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:07.520
The mythology that D one basketball

00:09:07.695 --> 00:09:12.060
and FBS football was just like

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.590
another extracurricular activity,

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:18.970
as if it were, you know, rowing, you know,

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.921
or this or the newspaper
or the drama club,

00:09:22.921 --> 00:09:27.921
when in fact, these have
become gigantic businesses

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:30.960
that are completely independent

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.693
of the educational
mission of these schools.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.080
And in college sports,

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.927
there was not only the argument
that if we pay the players,

00:09:39.927 --> 00:09:41.655
it will destroy the sport.

00:09:41.655 --> 00:09:43.439
This is Gabe Feldman.

00:09:43.439 --> 00:09:47.210
He's a professor of Sports
Law at Tulane University

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:50.830
and overall expert on all things NCAA.

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.670
There was this added layer

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.277
that if we pay the players,

00:09:54.277 --> 00:09:57.970
that will mean that much
of the benefit will flow

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.970
to a relatively small number of players,

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.150
and the other sports will be hurt.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.960
So we'll end up with college football

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.950
and maybe some men's
and women's basketball,

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:09.020
but rowing and swimming and
diving and golf and lacrosse,

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.000
that'll all collapse

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.483
because schools are
struggling to make money.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.300
So if the pandemic laid
bare the importance

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.060
of college football revenue,

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:22.299
and the NCAA stood firm
on not paying players,

00:10:22.299 --> 00:10:24.630
could there be a middle ground?

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:25.463
I think there were plenty

00:10:25.463 --> 00:10:26.820
of people who bought that argument,

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.840
accepted that we will do damage
to college sports as a whole

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.320
if we provide compensation

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.870
for football and basketball players.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.730
And so name, image, and likeness rights

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.750
were seen as I think, a compromise,

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.530
as a workable solution
that would provide benefits

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:41.850
for college athletes.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.080
Compensation for name,
image, and likeness,

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.869
or NIL, doesn't involve
colleges paying players

00:10:46.869 --> 00:10:49.480
but does allow athletes to benefit

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:50.930
from their accomplishments

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:53.200
as the schools, conferences, TV networks

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.250
and others already are.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.560
Help me understand how NIL fits

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:00.424
into sort of the larger argument.

00:11:00.424 --> 00:11:03.271
NIL, for the benefit of those watching,

00:11:03.271 --> 00:11:05.930
are the rights to exploit

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:08.030
an athlete's name, image, and likeness,

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.510
and the payment for those rights

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:13.950
doesn't really come from the schools.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.470
And that's one of the
reasons these schools should

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.920
not be upset about NIL.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:19.885
It doesn't come out of their pocket.

00:11:19.885 --> 00:11:24.420
It's like, it's like, you're
making the players happy,

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.100
and you don't have to pay anything.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.710
No institutional involvement,

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.740
no direct or even indirect
money from the colleges.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.350
Originally the NIL debate

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.920
focused around avatars
in EA Sports video games.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.430
In EA NCAA Basketball 09,

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:41.141
an unnamed UCLA player looked exactly like

00:11:41.141 --> 00:11:44.017
and played the same
position as Ed O'Bannon,

00:11:44.017 --> 00:11:46.391
a former UCLA basketball player.

00:11:46.391 --> 00:11:49.540
This led to O'Bannon v. NCAA,

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.480
an antitrust class action lawsuit

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.250
filed against the NCAA
that challenged their use

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.855
of its athletes likeness
for commercial purposes.

00:11:56.855 --> 00:11:58.940
I don't think we can overlook the impact

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:01.000
that Ed O'Bannon has had on this.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.120
When he filed his lawsuit
to try to get compensation

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.850
for former and current college athletes,

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:09.520
he lost the case, at least
as it pertained to NIL,

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.894
but I think he showed a path forward.

00:12:11.894 --> 00:12:15.114
And I think he helped
start to convince people

00:12:15.114 --> 00:12:19.407
that it's possible to both
save and protect college sports

00:12:19.407 --> 00:12:21.260
and maybe even strengthen college sports,

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:25.217
while also providing more
rights for college athletes.

00:12:25.217 --> 00:12:27.100
While the Supreme Court ultimately

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.270
declined to hear the O'Bannon case,

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:31.570
the focus for NIL has now shifted away

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:35.350
from video games and towards social media.

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:38.800
It's hard to ignore that
this is no longer just

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.180
about the traditional endorsement.

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:44.150
This is no longer just
about the star athlete

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.490
getting the deal with the apparel company.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:48.820
And the opportunities that has provided

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.500
for young people in
this innovation economy.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.110
When an athlete comes on campus,

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.770
and they have more than
a million followers,

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:59.960
why can't they then
monetize their popularity?

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.020
If you are a musician,

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:03.280
you could record something,

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.440
and you can get, Hey,
everyone else at the school

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.713
is allowed to take advantage

00:13:09.713 --> 00:13:11.722
if you have those opportunities

00:13:11.722 --> 00:13:16.722
except the athletes who
want to play NCAA sports.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.247
Katie Ledecky, probably
the most famous swimmer

00:13:19.247 --> 00:13:20.893
in the country right now,

00:13:20.893 --> 00:13:25.893
actually had to resign
from competing any longer

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:28.897
on the Stanford swimming team

00:13:28.897 --> 00:13:33.570
because the loss of her potential revenues

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.213
got to a point where, in
consultation with her family,

00:13:37.213 --> 00:13:40.317
she just said, she couldn't
give it up any longer.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.290
There should be some
requirements for transparency,

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:46.439
and institutions should not be involved

00:13:46.439 --> 00:13:48.025
in setting up these deals.

00:13:48.025 --> 00:13:50.836
And there should be no use
of the institutional mark.

00:13:50.836 --> 00:13:54.240
Because again, this is about
allowing the individuals

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:59.240
as athletes to be able to
benefit from their name,

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:02.199
image, and likeness, not
that of the university.

00:14:02.199 --> 00:14:04.870
One thing NIL and directly
paying the players

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:08.100
have in common is the NCAA
has fought against both

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:09.830
for decades.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:11.600
So why do you think they resisted so much?

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:12.860
Because that's the system?

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.100
They resisted because that's the way

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.100
it's always been.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.337
They do worry about the disparity between

00:14:17.337 --> 00:14:20.497
the quarterback getting a car ad

00:14:20.497 --> 00:14:23.460
and the center getting nothing.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:24.860
The reality is,

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.930
and that's what our
antitrust laws establish,

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:32.930
is competition leads generally
to more fan interest,

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:38.270
more revenues, and a fair
allocation of the revenues

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:40.793
to the people who actually generate them.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:43.040
And over the past few years,

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.060
the fight for NIL has gotten messy.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:49.421
With no action by the NCAA
and no national standard,

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:52.500
individual states chose to step in.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.070
In September, 2019,

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:57.650
California initiated the
Fair Pay to Play Act.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.705
That allowed athletes to be paid

00:14:59.705 --> 00:15:02.073
for their name, image and likeness.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.900
Other states like Florida followed suit.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:06.061
And on top of all that,

00:15:06.061 --> 00:15:08.650
the Supreme Court has heard oral arguments

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:12.060
in the pivotal NCAA versus Alston case,

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:13.420
with judges making arguments

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.533
in both directions and some
staying above the fray.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:18.830
It's a tough case for me.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.547
This is not an ordinary product.

00:15:20.547 --> 00:15:23.623
So I worry a lot about judges
getting into the business

00:15:23.623 --> 00:15:26.953
of deciding how amateur
sport should be run.

00:15:27.881 --> 00:15:30.110
One of the things that it's hard for

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:32.762
I think an outsider to
totally comprehend is

00:15:32.762 --> 00:15:35.160
you've got California, you've got Florida,

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.978
you've got the Supreme
Court, you've got the NCAA.

00:15:37.978 --> 00:15:40.618
When you cut through it,

00:15:40.618 --> 00:15:43.850
where does this really
kind of come down to?

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:45.760
Like who ultimately is going to be sort

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:47.810
of the decider here?

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:51.180
There is a legitimate
reason why you want one set

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.440
of NIL rules, right?

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:54.800
Because otherwise you really get

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:59.360
into the thing where 50 states
have 50 different rules.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:00.940
And it's a, it's a mess.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:04.200
We are dealing with a
situation I've compared

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:07.340
to the NCAA is now juggling fiery knives.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.519
And the knives that are up in the air

00:16:09.519 --> 00:16:13.533
are an amateurism antitrust decision.

00:16:13.533 --> 00:16:16.830
There's activity at the state
level, the federal level,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.260
the Supreme Court level.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.160
There are lawsuits still being filed.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.750
There's the NIL legislation.

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.400
There's the public pressure.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.490
And there's frankly not even agreement

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.240
within the NCAA about what
the right path forward is.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:31.800
You have recruiting advantages, you know,

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.740
based on what your NIL
is in California versus

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.800
what your NIL is in Maryland.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:39.740
The most logical place
for this to be sorted out

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:41.290
is Congress.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.310
I want to save college sports,

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:45.054
but I don't necessarily think

00:16:45.054 --> 00:16:49.510
that kids shouldn't get any
share of the money that is

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:53.765
increasingly making people in
this industry millionaires.

00:16:53.765 --> 00:16:56.480
That's Senator Chris
Murphy from Connecticut,

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.390
a diehard UCONN fan, and one
of the most outspoken members

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:00.720
of Congress when it comes to NIL.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:03.560
And that's why I've introduced

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:07.780
legislation that would create
a federal right to make money

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:08.827
off of your name, image, and likeness.

00:17:08.827 --> 00:17:11.810
And that's why I'm supportive
of broader efforts that would,

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:13.770
you know, create some federal expectations

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:17.545
around broader revenue
sharing with student athletes.

00:17:17.545 --> 00:17:19.282
Senator Murphy isn't the only

00:17:19.282 --> 00:17:21.550
member of Congress taking on NIL.

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:23.021
His co-sponsor in the
House is Representative

00:17:23.021 --> 00:17:25.170
Lori Trahan of Massachusetts,

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.600
who herself is a former
D one volleyball player

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:29.110
at Georgetown.

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:29.996
Go Hoyas.

00:17:29.996 --> 00:17:31.620
Another bill in Congress,

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.560
the College Athletes Bill of Rights,

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.152
was introduced by Cory Booker
and Richard Blumenthal,

00:17:36.152 --> 00:17:38.992
both Democrats from New
Jersey and Connecticut,

00:17:38.992 --> 00:17:40.020
respectively.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.972
It seeks, quote, fair and
equitable compensation

00:17:42.972 --> 00:17:44.729
for college athletes.

00:17:44.729 --> 00:17:47.324
We reached out to the NCAA for comment,

00:17:47.324 --> 00:17:49.772
and they directed us
to a statement reading:

00:17:49.772 --> 00:17:53.050
the board of directors
remains fully committed

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:54.540
to providing student athletes

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.797
with appropriate opportunities
within the collegiate model

00:17:57.797 --> 00:18:01.040
by modernizing division one
rules related to name, image

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.100
and likeness and transfer eligibility

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.019
at the first practical opportunity.

00:18:06.019 --> 00:18:09.616
If we're going to make a
good case for Congress,

00:18:09.616 --> 00:18:13.160
they need to understand
that all the universities

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:17.010
within the NCAA want
to see the same outcome

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:18.980
because timing is critical here

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:21.730
because we're frankly at a
point where the first state law

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:23.164
will go into effect in July,

00:18:23.164 --> 00:18:25.900
and we're leading toward chaos.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:27.280
That's not going to be good

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:29.184
for athletes or the universities.

00:18:29.184 --> 00:18:31.450
And so what do you worry about

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:32.440
kind of getting in the way?

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.772
What are the obstacles
here to getting this done?

00:18:34.772 --> 00:18:37.220
This industry is incredibly powerful.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:38.570
I think that, you know,

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:41.950
the power five schools are
not going to be interested

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:43.690
in changing the status quo.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:47.230
And I think that's largely
because the power now exists

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:49.740
in individual school athletic programs

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:53.213
and individual conferences,
not in the NCAA.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:55.683
And he's right.

00:18:55.683 --> 00:18:56.860
The power in decision making

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:59.880
for allocating revenue
has shifted from the NCAA

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.530
to what's called the power five,

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.980
or the five most prestigious
athletic conferences

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:05.240
in America.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:08.353
And one of the things
that a lot of sports fans,

00:19:08.353 --> 00:19:11.540
college sports fans, for example,

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:15.520
don't really understand is
that the NCAA does not control

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.240
the college football playoff.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:20.330
And the $500 million a year
generated by the college

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:24.227
football playoff is handled
independently of the NCAA.

00:19:24.227 --> 00:19:27.652
The FBS institutions receive that funding.

00:19:27.652 --> 00:19:30.700
This fits into sort of some even bigger

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:34.510
questions it feels like
around how colleges operate,

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:35.890
how they spend their money.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:38.632
It's long overdue to have a conversation

00:19:38.632 --> 00:19:43.632
about recalibrating revenue
and costs in college sports.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:46.140
Also recalibrating the
governance structure

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:47.776
in division one sports,

00:19:47.776 --> 00:19:49.750
and the Knight Commission has been looking

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:51.207
into these issues for a very long time

00:19:51.207 --> 00:19:52.493
and continues to do so.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.250
There's significant opportunities here

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:59.236
because the growth in the media revenues

00:19:59.236 --> 00:20:01.440
is not going to go away.

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:02.610
We've seen over time,

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.824
it continues to increase
in college sports.

00:20:04.824 --> 00:20:08.770
Some of the challenges really
are a result of its success

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:10.320
and media revenues,

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.010
but we haven't met the moment

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:15.333
of the challenges by changing the system,

00:20:15.333 --> 00:20:17.870
by changing the incentives,

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:19.870
to really ensure that those revenues

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.254
are going to the right places

00:20:21.254 --> 00:20:25.830
and going to support a healthy
system across all sports,

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:27.480
not just football and basketball.

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:30.920
While much has made of how much money

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:32.656
pours into and swirls around

00:20:32.656 --> 00:20:35.140
big time college football and basketball,

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.737
Senator Murphy and other
proponents of NIL reform

00:20:37.737 --> 00:20:41.210
say the biggest beneficiaries
may not be the star athletes,

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:43.350
like Trevor Lawrence and Zion Williamson,

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:44.828
who would simply be getting a down payment

00:20:44.828 --> 00:20:46.850
on a future fortune.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.850
The real winners may be athletes

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:50.660
at top tier college programs,

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:53.400
like the fabled UCONN
Women's Basketball team,

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.960
some of who may ultimately
eke out a living playing

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.420
professional basketball,

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.450
but whose best chance of being compensated

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.913
for their talents may come in college.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:04.724
Managing a vaccine rollout

00:21:04.724 --> 00:21:07.050
and an ensuing economic recovery

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.160
will likely push Senator Murphy's bill

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:12.260
and other legislation
until later in the year.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.730
In the meantime, we'll
get a look at how athletes

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.730
may or may not benefit
from state legislation.

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:21.520
The NCAA may opt to take
this issue up again,

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:24.940
and the Supreme Court will
likely issue its ruling

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:25.908
by the summer.

00:21:25.908 --> 00:21:28.824
2021 may bring some semblance of change,

00:21:28.824 --> 00:21:32.150
or it could bring an
entirely new business model

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:33.468
to college sports,

00:21:33.468 --> 00:21:36.575
one that actually includes the athletes.

00:21:36.575 --> 00:21:38.220
Thanks so much for watching.

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:39.560
Would love to hear what you think

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:43.350
about this and other episodes
about the business of sports.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.543
I'll be in the comment section.

00:21:44.543 --> 00:21:46.243
Can't wait to hear what you think.

